Important: Presence of physical content alone does not prove the update is recognized as downloaded, correctly associated with its deployment package, and available to clients. A failed Primary-initiated deployment suggests a functional problem, not necessarily a cosmetic console issue.
2. First identify which status is wrong
1. On both CAS and Primary consoles, open Software Library > Software Updates > All Software Updates. Locate the same 25H2 feature update, including architecture, language and revision. Compare the Downloaded column.
2. Open the update Properties > Content Information, where available, and compare the referenced content information and status. Record the CI Unique ID, content identifiers and revision using supported console/PowerShell methods.
3. Independently check Monitoring > Distribution Status > Content Status for the deployment package containing the update. Note the package ID, source path, and each DP distribution state.
4. Confirm whether the failed Primary-initiated deployment fails at package creation/download/distribution, policy delivery, or client download. Collect the exact error code and relevant timestamps.
These are distinct states: update-level Downloaded, deployment package content, and DP distribution. Do not conflate them.
3. Verify database replication between CAS and Primary
5. In the console, navigate to Monitoring > Database Replication. Inspect the CAS-to-Primary replication link and associated replication groups.
6. Check whether replication is Active, Degraded, or Failed. Compare the timestamps with the last attempted content download or package change.
7. Review RCMCtrl.log on both sites for replication backlog or errors. Where applicable, check replmgr.log and objreplmgr.log for relevant replication processing.
8. If the link is unhealthy, use Replication Link Analyzer and follow supported repair guidance with Microsoft support. Avoid direct SQL modifications.
A healthy overall replication link does not guarantee that a particular update/content association is consistent. Treat replication as one hypothesis to test, not a confirmed diagnosis.
4. Validate the deployment package and content
9. In Monitoring > Distribution Status > Content Status, locate the specific deployment package containing the 25H2 feature update. Record package ID and DP status.
10. In Software Library > Application Management > Packages or Software Updates > Deployment Packages, inspect the deployment package source path and package membership. Confirm the source is reachable by the site server computer account.
11. Use the supported Validate action on affected DPs, where available, and review resulting status messages. Validation checks DP content integrity; it does not directly repair the update-level Downloaded flag.
12. If validation fails, inspect missing files, access permissions and hash failures before redistributing. Use Content Library Explorer for content-library inspection if appropriate.
13. Compare the exact same deployment package and content IDs between CAS and Primary; avoid assuming that similar 25H2 titles reference the same update revision.
