8. Recommended execution order
14. Capture CAS and Primary screenshots for the same update and its properties, including update revision and exact Downloaded column.
15. Collect update CI/Content IDs, deployment package ID, affected DP and failure error code.
16. Check Database Replication status and RCMCtrl.log on both sites.
17. Validate package source and distribution status, then inspect distmgr.log and pkgxfermgr.log for the matching Package ID.
18. Review PatchDownloader.log for a controlled, authorized test download if necessary.
19. Repair only the confirmed failing layer; retest update-level status and a small pilot Primary-initiated deployment.
20. If the inconsistency persists, attach evidence to the already opened Microsoft support case and request analysis of update-content state and CAS–Primary replication.
9. Safety and operational cautions
• Do not directly update Configuration Manager SQL tables or manually toggle a Downloaded flag; this is unsupported and may corrupt site state.
• Do not use CMUpdateReset.exe to reset a Windows 11 25H2 feature update; it targets Configuration Manager in-console servicing updates.
• Do not indiscriminately delete source directories or purge the content library. Preserve evidence before any changes.
• Continue the working CAS deployment path temporarily only if it is approved and is distributing content reliably to the expected clients.
• Avoid repeated mass redistributions until the failing stage is identified.
10. Suggested Microsoft case question
“Why does our CAS recognize the Windows 11 25H2 update content as downloaded and successfully initiate deployments, while the Primary Site reports Downloaded = No and Primary-initiated deployments fail? The issue persists after our Configuration Manager site upgrade. Please help determine whether this is update metadata, content/package association, database replication, or another supported content-state issue, and advise a supported repair.”
